\title{A Formal Logical Skeleton of Geometric Complexity Theory:\\
Obstruction Maps, Orbit-Closure Non-Containment, and an\\
Algebraic Natural-Proofs Barrier}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 21, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Geometric Complexity Theory (GCT), introduced by Mulmuley and Sohoni, recasts
central lower-bound questions of algebraic complexity --- most famously the
conjectured separation of the permanent from the determinant --- as questions of
\emph{non-containment of orbit closures} in spaces of polynomials, to be resolved
by exhibiting \emph{representation-theoretic obstructions}. We present a fully
axiomatic, machine-checkable formalization of the logical skeleton of this
program. We isolate five axioms that any model of GCT must satisfy: orbit-closure
containment is a preorder; orbit dimension is monotone under containment; small
circuits imply containment in low-dimensional orbits; representation multiplicities
are well-defined; and (Schur domination) containment forces pointwise multiplicity
domination. From these we derive the fundamental theorem that a single multiplicity
gap obstructs containment, and we build it into a circuit lower-bound bridge: a
target object having an obstruction against every low-dimensional competitor must
have super-budget circuit size. We further formalize a dimension-to-circuit
estimate, transitivity and composition lemmas, a self-consistency theorem (no
object obstructs itself), and --- turning the framework on itself --- an
\emph{algebraic natural-proofs barrier}: any sound separator that uses only
bounded-weight representations and that succeeds against a hard class whose
multiplicities are supported on exponentially-high weights must itself use
representations of exponentially large weight. All results are stated as theorems
with complete proofs and correspond to mechanically verified Lean~4 declarations.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The flagship open problem of computational complexity, $P \stackrel{?}{=} NP$, has
an algebraic avatar --- Valiant's $\mathrm{VP} \stackrel{?}{=} \mathrm{VNP}$ ---
which asks whether the permanent polynomial can be expressed as a polynomial-size
determinant (up to affine substitution and padding). The relativization,
algebrization, and natural-proofs barriers showed that broad families of proof
techniques cannot settle such separations. Geometric Complexity Theory (GCT) is a
response: it proposes that the separation is \emph{geometric}, encoded in whether
the orbit closure of one polynomial under the general linear group contains
another, and that non-containment can be \emph{certified} by representation theory.

Two features make GCT attractive and, simultaneously, hard. First, it converts an
analytic/geometric impossibility (one variety not lying inside another) into a
discrete search for an \emph{obstruction}: an irreducible representation occurring
with higher multiplicity in the coordinate ring of one object than the other.
Second, the framework appears, optimistically, to evade the natural-proofs barrier
--- but only if the requisite obstructions are \emph{small enough to write down}.

This paper formalizes the load-bearing logic of GCT and, crucially, also
formalizes the obstacle. We make no claim to compute multiplicities of the
determinant or permanent; rather, we prove that the \emph{inferential machinery} of
GCT is sound and self-consistent, and that an internal barrier governs the size of
the certificates it requires. The development is axiomatic: we capture GCT as a
type class \texttt{GCTSystem} and prove all consequences abstractly, so that any
concrete instantiation (with genuine orbit closures and plethysm multiplicities)
inherits the theorems for free.

\section{Definitions}

We work over an abstract type $\alpha$ of \emph{objects} (intended model:
homogeneous polynomials, or points of a $\mathrm{GL}$-representation).

\begin{definition}[Representation index]
A \texttt{RepIndex} is a pair $\lambda = (\mathrm{label}, \wt)$ of natural numbers.
The intended model is a partition (Young diagram) labelling an irreducible
polynomial $\mathrm{GL}$-representation; $\wt$ corresponds to $|\lambda|$, the number
of boxes / the degree. Equality of indices is decidable.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[GCT system]\label{def:gct}
A \texttt{GCTSystem} on $\alpha$ consists of the following data and axioms.
\begin{itemize}
  \item A relation $\preceq$ (\texttt{inClosure}), where $f \preceq g$ means
        $f \in \Obar{g}$, satisfying
        \textbf{(Refl)} $f \preceq f$ and
        \textbf{(Trans)} $f \preceq g \wedge g \preceq h \Rightarrow f \preceq h$.
  \item A function $\dimo : \alpha \to \NN$ (\texttt{orbitDim}) with
        \textbf{(Mono)} $f \preceq g \Rightarrow \dimo(f) \le \dimo(g)$.
  \item A function $\size : \alpha \to \NN$ (\texttt{circuitSize}) with
        \textbf{(SmallCircuit)} for all $f, B$, if $\size(f) \le B$ then there
        exists $g$ with $f \preceq g$ and $\dimo(g) \le B^2$.
  \item A multiplicity function $\mult : \texttt{RepIndex} \to \alpha \to \NN$
        (\texttt{repMult}) with
        \textbf{(Schur)} $f \preceq g \Rightarrow \forall \lambda,\
        \mult(\lambda, f) \le \mult(\lambda, g)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
The (Schur) axiom is the formal shadow of the fact that, for a
$\mathrm{GL}$-stable degeneration $f \in \Obar{g}$, the coordinate ring of
$\Obar{f}$ is a quotient/limit whose isotypic multiplicities are dominated by those
of $\Obar{g}$. The (SmallCircuit) axiom abstracts the standard dimension count: an
object computed by a size-$B$ circuit lies in a constructible family whose closure
has dimension $O(B^2)$.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Obstruction witness]
For objects $f, g$, an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$ is a representation index
$\lambda$ together with a proof of $\mult(\lambda, f) > \mult(\lambda, g)$. Such a
$\lambda$ is an \emph{obstruction} certifying $f \notin \Obar{g}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Algebraic separator]
An \texttt{AlgSeparator} on $\alpha$ is a Boolean classifier
$\mathrm{classify} : \alpha \to \{\mathsf{true}, \mathsf{false}\}$ with a weight
ceiling $\maxW \in \NN$ such that, whenever $\mathrm{classify}(f) = \mathsf{true}$
and $\mathrm{classify}(g) = \mathsf{false}$:
\textbf{(Sound)} $f \not\preceq g$; and
\textbf{(BoundedReps)} there exists $\lambda$ with $\wt(\lambda) \le \maxW$ and
$\mult(\lambda, f) > \mult(\lambda, g)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hard class]
A \texttt{HardClassData} on $\alpha$ consists of families
$\mathrm{hard}, \mathrm{easy} : \NN \to \alpha$ and a constant $c := \mathrm{exp\_const} \ge 1$
such that
\textbf{(HardExpWeight)} for all $n \ge 1$ and all $\lambda$, if
$\mult(\lambda, \mathrm{hard}(n)) > 0$ then $\wt(\lambda) \ge 2^{\,c\,n}$.
\end{definition}

\section{Main results}

Throughout, $S$ denotes a fixed \texttt{GCTSystem} on $\alpha$.

\subsection{The obstruction principle}

\begin{theorem}[Obstruction implies non-containment]\label{thm:obs}
If there exists an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$, then $f \not\preceq g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let the witness be $\lambda$ with $\mult(\lambda, f) > \mult(\lambda, g)$. Suppose
$f \preceq g$. By (Schur), $\mult(\lambda, f) \le \mult(\lambda, g)$, contradicting
the strict inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Direct non-containment]\label{thm:direct}
If $\mult(\lambda, f) > \mult(\lambda, g)$ for some single $\lambda$, then
$f \not\preceq g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The hypothesis is exactly an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:obs}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No self-obstruction]\label{thm:noself}
The type \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,f$ is empty.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A witness would give $\mult(\lambda, f) > \mult(\lambda, f)$, which is impossible;
equivalently, by Theorem~\ref{thm:obs} it would yield $f \not\preceq f$,
contradicting (Refl).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Simultaneous non-containment]\label{thm:simul}
Given an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$ and an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,h$,
we have $f \not\preceq g$ and $f \not\preceq h$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:obs} to each witness.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Order-theoretic structure}

\begin{theorem}[Containment transitivity]\label{thm:trans}
If $f \preceq g$ and $g \preceq h$ then $f \preceq h$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is axiom (Trans).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicity-domination transitivity]\label{thm:multtrans}
If $\mult(\lambda,f) \le \mult(\lambda,g)$ for all $\lambda$, and
$\mult(\lambda,g) \le \mult(\lambda,h)$ for all $\lambda$, then
$\mult(\lambda,f) \le \mult(\lambda,h)$ for all $\lambda$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pointwise transitivity of $\le$ on $\NN$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No obstruction implies local domination]\label{thm:local}
Let $I$ be a finite set of representation indices. If for every $\lambda \in I$ it
is not the case that $\mult(\lambda,f) > \mult(\lambda,g)$, then
$\mult(\lambda,f) \le \mult(\lambda,g)$ for all $\lambda \in I$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each $\lambda \in I$, $\neg(a > b)$ on $\NN$ gives $a \le b$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Dimension and circuit lower bounds}

\begin{theorem}[Circuit from dimension]\label{thm:cfd}
If $\dimo(f) > B^2$ then $\size(f) > B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\size(f) \le B$. By (SmallCircuit) there is $g$ with $f \preceq g$ and
$\dimo(g) \le B^2$. By (Mono), $\dimo(f) \le \dimo(g) \le B^2$, contradicting
$\dimo(f) > B^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit-dimension lower bound]\label{thm:odlb}
If for every $g$ with $\dimo(g) \le D$ there is an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$,
then $\dimo(f) > D$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\dimo(f) \le D$. Apply the hypothesis to $g := f$, obtaining an
\texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,f$; by (Refl) $f \preceq f$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:obs} gives $f \not\preceq f$, a contradiction (cf.\
Theorem~\ref{thm:noself}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Circuit lower bound from obstructions]\label{thm:clb}
Fix a budget $B$. If for every $g$ with $\dimo(g) \le B^2$ there is an
\texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$, then $\size(f) > B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\size(f) \le B$. By (SmallCircuit) there is $g$ with $f \preceq g$ and
$\dimo(g) \le B^2$. The hypothesis supplies an \texttt{ObstructionWitness}\,$f\,g$,
whence by Theorem~\ref{thm:obs} $f \not\preceq g$ --- contradicting $f \preceq g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:clb} is the strategic core of GCT: it reduces a circuit lower
bound for $f$ to a catalog of representation-theoretic certificates, one
obstruction per low-dimensional competitor $g$. No reasoning about circuits remains
in the hypothesis; the entire burden is moved into representation theory.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The algebraic natural-proofs barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Algebraic natural-proofs barrier]\label{thm:barrier}
Let $\mathrm{sep}$ be an \texttt{AlgSeparator} and let $H$ be a \texttt{HardClassData}
with constant $c = \mathrm{exp\_const} \ge 1$. Fix $n \ge 1$ and suppose
$\mathrm{classify}(\mathrm{hard}(n)) = \mathsf{true}$ and
$\mathrm{classify}(\mathrm{easy}(n)) = \mathsf{false}$. Then
\[
  \maxW(\mathrm{sep}) \ \ge\ 2^{\,c\,n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By (BoundedReps), the separation yields $\lambda$ with $\wt(\lambda) \le \maxW$ and
$\mult(\lambda, \mathrm{hard}(n)) > \mult(\lambda, \mathrm{easy}(n))$. Since
multiplicities are non-negative,
$\mult(\lambda, \mathrm{hard}(n)) > \mult(\lambda, \mathrm{easy}(n)) \ge 0$, so
$\mult(\lambda, \mathrm{hard}(n)) > 0$. By (HardExpWeight) applied to this $\lambda$
(using $n \ge 1$), $\wt(\lambda) \ge 2^{cn}$. Chaining,
$\maxW \ge \wt(\lambda) \ge 2^{cn}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The barrier is the algebraic mirror of Razborov--Rudich. A separator is a
``natural'' certificate: sound, and constructive via low-weight obstructions. The
theorem says no such certificate of small weight ceiling can succeed against a
class whose multiplicities are concentrated at exponential weight. GCT therefore
does not evade the difficulty; it \emph{relocates} it into the question of whether
obstructions of feasible weight exist for genuine determinant/permanent instances.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithmic content}

Although the framework is axiomatic, its proofs are constructive enough to read off
algorithms for any decidable instantiation.

\begin{description}
  \item[Algorithm A (Finite obstruction search).] Given $f, g$ and a finite set
  $I$ of indices with computable $\mult$, scan $I$; if some $\lambda \in I$ has
  $\mult(\lambda,f) > \mult(\lambda,g)$, output $\lambda$ as a certificate of
  $f \not\preceq g$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:obs}); else report ``no obstruction in $I$,''
  which by Theorem~\ref{thm:local} certifies pointwise domination on $I$. Cost:
  $O(|I|)$ multiplicity evaluations.
  \item[Algorithm B (Lower bound by obstruction catalog).] Given $f$, budget $B$,
  and an enumeration of competitors $g$ with $\dimo(g) \le B^2$, run Algorithm~A on
  each $(f,g)$; if every competitor receives a certificate, conclude
  $\size(f) > B$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:clb}.
  \item[Algorithm C (Barrier weight estimator).] Given $c$ and $n$, return the
  certified lower bound $2^{cn}$ on any successful separator's weight ceiling
  (Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier}).
\end{description}

\section{Applications and discussion}

Theorem~\ref{thm:clb} reframes ``prove $f$ has no size-$B$ circuit'' as ``produce
one obstruction per low-dimensional competitor,'' the operational content of GCT and
the target of all multiplicity computations (plethysm, Kronecker,
Littlewood--Richardson) in the literature. Theorems~\ref{thm:noself}
and~\ref{thm:odlb} guarantee the obstruction calculus never derives the absurd
$f \not\preceq f$, eliminating a class of silent bugs in lower-bound software.
Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} tells implementers that, against hard classes, raising
$\maxW$ is unavoidable.

The relationship between Theorem~\ref{thm:clb} (the bridge) and
Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} (the barrier) is the intellectual heart of the paper. The
bridge promises that obstructions suffice; the barrier proves that, for the hardest
classes, those obstructions must be exponentially heavy. Together they delineate
where the remaining mathematical difficulty lives: in \emph{exhibiting} high-weight
obstructions for explicit instances, not in the logic that would deploy them.

\section{Future work}

A natural next step is to instantiate \texttt{GCTSystem} with a concrete model (e.g.\
a symmetric-function setting where multiplicities are genuine plethysm
coefficients) and verify the five axioms there. A second is to refine the barrier:
quantify how the weight ceiling scales with the \emph{number} of competitors in
Theorem~\ref{thm:clb}. A third is to port the framework to border-rank settings for
matrix multiplication, where the same preorder/domination pattern appears.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete, machine-verified logical skeleton for Geometric
Complexity Theory: five axioms, an obstruction principle, a circuit lower-bound
bridge, supporting order theory and consistency results, and an algebraic
natural-proofs barrier. The development isolates with certainty what is
\emph{easy} (the logic) from what remains \emph{hard} (the multiplicity
computations), and proves, inside the framework, why the hard part is hard.

